From 07e04adfc7e6ac9389211deed2b6f1e7527a5af6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:58:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: tell a film in the fields of interlaced video
 from its decoded pictures

A film on a DVD, and on television, is often stored as interlaced video. In 3:2 pulldown (NTSC)
four pictures of the film fill five coded frames, and a top and a bottom field repeat once in
every five; in 2:2 (PAL, and NTSC video at 30) each frame holds the two fields of one picture of
the film, or one field off; a film at 25 in 29.97 progressive frames repeats one frame in six.
None of it is in the flags of the stream: it is in the pictures.

FieldCadence.h compares the luma of each decoded picture with the one before: how much its top
and its bottom field changed, and how much it combs as coded and with its first field woven with
the second field of the picture before. A run of such labels is 3:2 where a top field repeats at
one place of the cycle of five and a bottom field at another, in nine cycles of ten and nowhere
else always (by parity, as an encoder may mark the frames of a pulldown top or bottom field
first); 25 in 30 where a whole frame repeats at one place of the cycle of six; 2:2 where nine
weaves in ten that tell are the frame's own (or one field off) and no field repeats alone; video
where pictures comb whichever way their fields are woven (a third of them, or six). A still, too
few cycles, or anything less clear tells nothing. Decide() takes the start of a stream and three
places further on: a film only where every place that told says the same film, never where one
says video. CCadenceWatch says, with hysteresis, where the cadence stops and comes back during
playback. GraphOf() gives the filters of FFmpeg that put the pictures back together (fieldmatch,
bwdif on the frames that no weave makes whole, decimate).

FieldCadenceProbe.h decodes a stretch of MPEG-2 video read before it is played with a decoder of
its own, and takes the video out of a stretch of a transport stream or of a Matroska file read
at a place of its own (the program stream already had its extractor).

The idea, and the use of fieldmatch and decimate, come from CorentinB's pull request 101 to
CroqueMr's build; the detection, the decision and the code are Hades' own.
